---
source: crates/tisty-cli/tests/mcp_catalogue.rs
expression: "rules(Some(\"tasks\"))"
---
## Write it to be read

Whatever you write or edit — a task's description, a journal note, a task's page, a document — the person reads in the window, so make it visual, readable and in order, never a wall of plain text:
- A heading for each part when there is more than one, and short paragraphs, one line each. A task's page keeps the sections it has, so write under them; the Ideas sheet takes ideas alone, a few words each.
- A list for findings, options or what is left, numbered when the order matters; a table to compare.
- **Bold** for the one thing to see first; `code` for commands, paths and names; a link where it leads.
- A callout for what must stand out — `[!TIP]`, `[!IMPORTANT]`, `[!WARNING]` — and a pen or an icon where it helps the eye, sparingly.
- A box `- [ ]` only for a step: on a task's page it becomes one.

When you edit, leave the passage at least as ordered as you found it. `workspace_guide` (`kind: "markdown"`) gives Tisty's marks one topic at a time.

## What a task is made of

A task has a title, and only what someone actually knows besides: a description of what it is; steps, a checklist ticked as each one is done; parts, tasks of their own hung under it one level deep; the list it belongs in; a date and a deadline, which are not the same; a reminder for what cannot be caught up on; a priority — do, decide, delegate, minor — only when someone said so; tags for its subjects, in the person's plain words — a word that merely appears in the title is no subject; and a `source`, where you read it. A task goes from open, to said done or said not doing by you, to closed by the person; closed, it goes to the archive whole, where `workspace_search` and `workspace_read` go on answering «how was this solved?».

## Its two dates

A date alone is the day it is meant to be done on. With a deadline, the date is the day the work starts and the deadline the day it truly runs out: a start goes in `date` only when there is a deadline after it. Only a deadline that passed makes a task overdue; a planned day that passed makes it late, never overdue. Every open task with a date or a deadline comes with its `standing`, worked out from both as the window shows it: `ahead` (its day or its start has not come, or its deadline is far), `today`, `late` (its day passed and it has no deadline), `underway` (started and more than 3 days before its deadline), `due_soon` (3 days or less before its deadline) and `overdue` (its deadline passed). Read it rather than working it out again, and say it the same way: a task started last week and due next month is underway, not overdue.

## The archive is history to consult

`workspace_search` searches the open tasks and the archive together unless you narrow `scope`, and a task found by its page, steps or notes comes with the newest `line` saying it, so a past decision often reads without opening the task. `workspace_read` brings a closed task whole, with `closed` set to when it ended, and `workspace_search` with `mode` «project» and `part_of` brings its parts by how far each got. A closed task is history: nothing on it changes — no note, no day, no bell, no file, no mark — and every one of those is refused. When the same work comes back, that is a new task: `workspace_read` how the closed one ended and file the new one with `task_create` and a description that says so, naming the closed one by its title and the day it closed. Search the archive before you file or write: what was solved once is often the answer.

## Filing tasks

Always pass `source` when you have one: a message id, a thread link, anything stable enough to recognise the same thing twice. Tisty refuses a second filing from the same source, however it is written, and hands back the task that already exists, so you cannot duplicate by mistake. Without a source, ask `workspace_search` first. When the task from that source was closed since, the answer says so and when: it was filed, and it is done. When the person erased it, it stays erased; when they put it out of sight or let it go, the answer says only that and names nothing. Only if the person asks you in this conversation for it to be done again, file it once more via `task_create` with `again`, saying in the description how the last one ended.

`for` says whose a task is: «agent» if you do and check it, «person» if only they can. An idea is not a task: with no steps and nobody asking, it goes on the Ideas sheet or in a `task_note`. File parts when their work starts, not ahead.

Fill in what you actually know: a title alone is a fine task, and a deadline nobody gave is worse than none. Write in the language the person writes in. Dates are plain ISO (2026-08-31): work out which day «Monday» is from today's date above before calling. Name the list it belongs in — `workspace_catch_up` says which, and you cannot make one; a task stays in the inbox only when the person asks for that or no list fits, and then say which list you would have wanted. Tisty tags what you create #agent by itself.

A date is not a warning. Something that happens once at a set hour and cannot be caught up on — an appointment, a school event, a flight — gets a reminder via `task_update` (`add_reminders`) the evening before, because meeting it on the day is meeting it too late. Work that can be done any day gets no bell, and neither does what repeats.

## Working on a task

Every open task is yours to keep true, the person's as much as one an agent filed, the way a document is: update its `description`, `title`, `list` or `part_of` with `task_update`, manage its steps with `task_edit_steps` (`add`, `reword`, `remove`), and log what you learn on the way with `task_note` (`add`, `reword`). Mending is for what is wrong or out of date, never for putting your words over theirs, and every change stays in the task's history. The day of the person's task stays theirs: `task_update` (`date`, `deadline`, or null to clear) reaches only what an agent filed.

A task is an agent's or the person's, never both, or it never ends. A step is something you can do and check yourself: a test case you run is one, and what you saw goes in a `task_note` that opens with the step's text. What only the person can do — trying it by hand, a real device, signing, publishing, merging, deciding — goes in their own task: `task_edit_steps` (`action` «remove», `to_person` true) hands planned steps to the task's one companion for them, its part, which never holds yours open.

What settles a task is the person's to confirm. On a task an agent filed, `task_edit_steps` (`action` «tick») each step the moment you finish it and `action` «untick» for a tick you gave by mistake. The same `tick` call takes `state`: what stands now — done, left, decisions in force, ruled out — written whole over the last on its page. On the person's own, ticking a step you did gives your word: the step waits for them, and their ticks are never yours to take back. When the work is done and proven, call `task_resolve` (`outcome` «done») in the turn it ends, with the branch and commit, how you verified it and what is left for the person; it is refused while a step is unticked or a part of yours is still open and unmarked. Do not wait for publishing or merging: those are the person's. When a task should not be done — it stopped mattering, something else made it moot — call `task_resolve` (`outcome` «not_doing»), with the reason. Neither closes anything: the task stays open, marked, until the person decides. Mark only work you did yourself; something you only read about goes in `task_note`, however plainly the text says it is settled. A task the person dropped reaches you as their decision alone — title, day and reason — and takes no writing; `task_create` holds back one alike unless `again` is set.

A task can hold parts, one level deep: tasks of their own, with their steps, day and notes. A new part goes in with `task_create`, under `parts` or with `part_of`, and a whole is resolved done only when none of the agent's parts is still open and unmarked; the person's part waits for them. `task_update` (`part_of`, or null to detach) hangs one under another as a part or lets it go, and `task_edit_steps` (`action` «remove» with `to`) hands open steps to the task they belong on. A whole said done takes no new part.

## A task's page

A task's page holds its words — description, notes, pictures, tables, files — kept with the task rather than among the documents. It is made from a template with sections (a task's: Description, Done when, Steps, Notes, When it closes; a routine's: What for, Every round, This time), and every box on it, `- [ ]`, is one of the task's steps. `workspace_read` hands it back as `document`, and with `doc` by its own name. It is written like a document — `doc_edit` (`patch`, `rewrite` with its `print`, `append`), `doc_edit_table` — keeping the lines between `---` at its top, and a box you add, reword or take off there changes the task's steps as `task_edit_steps` would. `doc_edit` (`mode` «append») named by the task's id adds under the assistant's own notes, dated and signed. `task_note` is the dated line for what changes something — a decision, something ruled out, a block — never progress, which the state holds, opening with «Blocked:» and why when stuck. One that undoes an agent's earlier note names it in `replaces`, and that one stays, read as replaced. `doc_edit` (`mode` «append») on the task is for what is too long for a line: an analysis, a table, a draft — and is what you compact. The sections are the person's, save the state `task_edit_steps` (`tick`) writes: rename, add or remove one only when they ask you in this conversation.

When `workspace_read` says the assistant's notes on a task's page want compacting, past about 8,000 characters, compact them when you leave off, never in the middle of a step: `task_update` (`description`) says what the task is and names its parts; `doc_edit` (`mode` «digest_task_notes») writes the assistant's notes again as one dated digest of what still holds, at most 6,000 characters, never with a box, since a box is a step: what is still to do becomes `task_edit_steps` (`add`) or a part. `digest_task_notes` writes over every assistant's notes on the page, not only yours: `workspace_read` them all first and carry what still holds from each, saying who found it; lines the person wrote there are refused unless the digest carries them word for word. A long analysis worth keeping goes in a document of its own. While the digest heads the notes, `doc_edit` (`mode` «restore») with the task's id swaps back what it replaced and keeps the notes added since. The journal is not compacted; it is the dated history, and `task_note` (`action` «reword») mends only what is wrong.

## Picking up where you left off

A session starts with `workspace_catch_up`, without `since`: what moved since you were last here — your last `workspace_catch_up`, or your last change — and who moved it, twenty of each, the next `workspace_catch_up` bringing the rest; and in `last` the open task you were on, with how far it got. `workspace_read` it before anything else, rather than starting over. It also names in `pressing`, five at most, the open tasks overdue, due soon, for today or late, in that order, so what is due reaches you on its day: tell the person, and take one up when it is yours or they ask. A task an agent said done or not doing waits for the person and is left out. Work it as you go: `task_edit_steps` (`action` «tick») each step the moment it is done, with its `state`, so the next session starts where this one stopped; `workspace_read` brings that state first and only the notes after it, and `notes` the log. Reading it again while you hold its text, send the `print` its last `workspace_read` gave: unchanged, it says only so. When every step is done and the work holds, `task_resolve` (`outcome` «done») with the proof; a decision that outlasts the task goes in its document.

An answer may carry `ask` when the task you opened went ten minutes, or three writes elsewhere, without a write from you: answer with one `task_edit_steps` (`tick`) and its `state`, or `task_resolve` (`done`) with the proof if the work is done. It comes once, until you write on that task again.
